            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "MADE3",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "For simulating atmospheric aerosol particles and their effects on clouds and radiation, we use a configuration that includes the aerosol submodel MADE346 (3rd version of the Modal Aerosol Dynamics model for Europe adapted for global applications) coupled to the EMAC cloud and radiation submodels16,47,53 on the basis of the calculated aerosol particle number concentration, size distribution and chemical composition.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "QCTM/TAGGING configuration",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To calculate the effects of transport emissions on ozone and on the lifetime of atmospheric methane, EMAC is applied using the so-called QCTM/TAGGING configuration\u00b2\u00b9, including a very detailed description of the atmospheric gas-phase chemistry.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "TAGGING submodel",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The TAGGING submodel55,56 allows for quantifications of the contributions of specific emission sources, such as the transport sectors, to the concentrations of atmospheric chemical compounds.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "perturbation method",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The effects of transport-induced aerosols are calculated by a perturbation method, which is based on the analysis of the differences between simulations with and without the emissions of the respective transport sector.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "nudging technique",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To ensure statistically significant detection of even small transport-induced differences, the so-called nudging technique is applied, where meteorological model variables are relaxed towards meteorological reanalysis data, to minimize the differences due to their variation between the model simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "AirClim",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "tagging approach",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To calculate the transport-induced CH4 and CO2 effects, we applied the climate response model AirClim50,59,60.\nTo adapt the model to the specific requirements of this study, we enabled to take into account the results from the tagging approach as described in the following.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "ERF",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "For the aerosol effects, the forcing is derived as the difference in top-of-atmosphere radiative fluxes from two model runs, one with and one without the aerosol effects of the respective transport sector emissions16. Since this difference also includes the effects of rapid adjustments, for instance of clouds, it is regarded here as ERF16,71.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EMAC",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "SARF",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "In case of the ozone effect, the model calculations described above yield a stratospheric adjusted radiative forcing42,43 (SARF) by means of the multiple radiation calling technique using the transport-induced ozone contributions determined via the tagging approach15.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
